{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In unsuccessful edits, examples that retain the Murcko scaffold have a significantly lower average target_delta than those that change the scaffold, indicating scaffold preservation reduces property change magnitude in failed attempts.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare mean target_delta between scaffold‐retained and scaffold‐changed groups in the unsuccessful dataset (train_neg.parquet) and test for a significant difference.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train_neg.parquet into a DataFrame.\n2. Drop rows where target_delta is null.\n3. Split into two subsets based on murcko_scaffold_retained == True vs False.\n4. Compute the mean and standard deviation of target_delta in each subset.\n5. Perform a two‐sample t‐test (or Welch’s t‐test if variances differ) to assess the difference in means.\n6. Visualize the distributions with side‐by‐side boxplots or violin plots.",  
      "deliverables":"- Mean and standard deviation of target_delta for scaffold‐retained and scaffold‐changed groups.\n- Test statistic, degrees of freedom, and p‐value from the t‐test.\n- Boxplot or violin plot comparing the two distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For successful edits, the change in molecular weight (MW_delta) differs significantly between tasks aiming to increase water solubility (task_id=101) and those aiming to increase drug-likeness (task_id=103), reflecting different structural modification strategies.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether MW_delta distributions differ between task 101 (water solubility) and task 103 (drug-likeness) in train.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null MW_delta.\n2. Filter rows where task_id is 101 or 103.\n3. Extract MW_delta arrays for each task.\n4. Check normality (e.g., Shapiro‐Wilk) and variance homogeneity (Levene’s test).\n5. Depending on assumptions, run either an independent two‐sample t‐test or a Mann–Whitney U test.\n6. Plot density curves or boxplots of MW_delta for both tasks for visual comparison.",  
      "deliverables":"- Sample size, mean, and variance of MW_delta for task 101 and task 103.\n- Results of normality and variance tests.\n- Test statistic and p‐value from the chosen statistical test.\n- Overlaid density plot or boxplot comparing MW_delta distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, longer natural language prompts (measured by word count) correlate positively with larger absolute changes in molecular weight (|MW_delta|), suggesting more detailed instructions yield greater structural modifications.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the relationship between prompt length and the magnitude of MW_delta in train.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null prompt or MW_delta.\n2. Compute a new column prompt_length = number of words in the prompt (split on whitespace).\n3. Compute abs_MW_delta = absolute value of MW_delta.\n4. Calculate Pearson and Spearman correlation coefficients between prompt_length and abs_MW_delta.\n5. Fit a simple linear regression model predicting abs_MW_delta from prompt_length and check significance of the slope.\n6. Generate a scatter plot with a regression line and confidence band.",  
      "deliverables":"- Pearson’s r, Spearman’s rho, and corresponding p‐values.\n- Regression slope, intercept, and p‐value.\n- Scatter plot of prompt_length vs abs_MW_delta with regression line."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Among successful edits, replace operations yield a higher mean target_delta than insert or delete operations, suggesting replacements are more effective at optimizing the target property.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare mean target_delta across action_type categories (insert, replace, delete) in train.parquet and test for overall differences.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null target_delta.\n2. Group data by action_type and compute group means and variances.\n3. Conduct a one‐way ANOVA to test for differences in means across the three action types.\n4. If ANOVA is significant, perform a post‐hoc Tukey HSD test to identify which pairs differ.\n5. Create a boxplot or bar chart with error bars showing mean target_delta by action_type.",  
      "deliverables":"- Group means and standard deviations of target_delta for each action_type.\n- ANOVA F‐statistic, degrees of freedom, and p‐value.\n- Post‐hoc pairwise comparison results.\n- Boxplot or bar chart of target_delta by action_type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Unsuccessful delete operations lead to a more negative average QED_delta compared to unsuccessful replace operations, indicating deletions are more detrimental to drug-likeness when the edit fails.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether mean QED_delta differs between delete and replace actions in the unsuccessful dataset (train_neg.parquet).",  
      "steps":"1. Load train_neg.parquet and drop rows where QED_delta is null.\n2. Filter to action_type 'delete' and 'replace'.\n3. Compute the mean and standard deviation of QED_delta for each group.\n4. Assess normality and equal variances; conduct either a two‐sample t‐test or Mann–Whitney U test.\n5. Visualize the comparison using violin or boxplots.",  
      "deliverables":"- Means and standard deviations of QED_delta for delete and replace.\n- Test statistic and p‐value from the selected test.\n- Violin or boxplot comparing QED_delta distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Successful edits for task_id=103 (increase drug-likeness) retain the Murcko scaffold at a higher rate than unsuccessful edits for the same task, suggesting scaffold preservation contributes to success in optimizing drug-likeness.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the proportion of murcko_scaffold_retained between successful (train.parquet) and unsuccessful (train_neg.parquet) examples for task_id=103.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet.\n2. Filter both datasets to rows where task_id == 103.\n3. For each subset, count the number of True vs False in murcko_scaffold_retained and compute proportions.\n4. Perform a chi‐square test of independence on the 2×2 contingency table.\n5. Create a grouped bar chart showing retention rates for successful vs unsuccessful examples.",  
      "deliverables":"- Contingency table and retention proportions for each group.\n- Chi‐square statistic, degrees of freedom, and p‐value.\n- Bar chart comparing scaffold retention rates."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Edits described with longer text (greater character length in the 'edit' field) are less likely to succeed, implying more complex edits reduce success probability.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Model the relationship between edit description length and edit success across both datasets.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet, add a binary column success = 1 for train, 0 for train_neg.\n2. Compute edit_length = number of characters in the edit description for all rows.\n3. Combine both datasets into one DataFrame.\n4. Fit a logistic regression model predicting success from edit_length, controlling for task_id (as categorical dummy variables).\n5. Examine the regression coefficient for edit_length and its p‐value.\n6. Plot observed success rate vs edit_length binned (e.g., deciles) with a trend line.",  
      "deliverables":"- Logistic regression summary table (coefficients, standard errors, p‐values).\n- Odds ratio for edit_length.\n- Binned success rate plot showing relationship with edit_length."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Examples with missing target_delta exhibit significantly smaller absolute changes in synthetic accessibility (|SA_delta|) than examples with available target_delta, suggesting missing target measurements correspond to minor edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the magnitude of SA_delta between rows with missing vs non‐missing target_delta in train.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet.\n2. Create two groups: rows where target_delta is null, and rows where target_delta is not null.\n3. Drop any rows with null SA_delta.\n4. Compute abs_SA_delta = absolute value of SA_delta for both groups.\n5. Calculate group means and variances of abs_SA_delta.\n6. Perform a two‐sample t‐test or Mann–Whitney U test to compare the groups.\n7. Visualize with side‐by‐side boxplots or density plots.",  
      "deliverables":"- Mean and variance of abs_SA_delta for missing vs non‐missing target_delta groups.\n- Test statistic and p‐value from the chosen test.\n- Boxplot or density plot comparing the distributions."  
    }  
  }  
]}
